# Cloudflare Think Agentic Loop Research

## Source checkout

Cloudflare Agents was cloned into the project as a shallow local source mirror:

- Local path: `.agent-sources/cloudflare-agents`
- Repo: `https://github.com/cloudflare/agents.git`
- Observed commit: `6fa7fd7`
- Keep out of commits: `.agent-sources/` is excluded in `.git/info/exclude`.

Files read for this pass:

- `.agent-sources/cloudflare-agents/docs/think/index.md`
- `.agent-sources/cloudflare-agents/docs/think/getting-started.md`
- `.agent-sources/cloudflare-agents/docs/think/tools.md`
- `.agent-sources/cloudflare-agents/docs/think/sub-agents.md`
- `.agent-sources/cloudflare-agents/docs/think/programmatic-submissions.md`
- `.agent-sources/cloudflare-agents/docs/think/lifecycle-hooks.md`
- `.agent-sources/cloudflare-agents/docs/think/client-tools.md`
- `.agent-sources/cloudflare-agents/examples/assistant/README.md`
- `.agent-sources/cloudflare-agents/packages/think/src/think.ts`

## Bottom line

Cloudflare Think is not just a chat wrapper. It is close to the agentic loop we need for the D&D prototype.

Think provides:

- Durable Object-backed agent identity and SQLite state.
- A default agentic loop around AI SDK `streamText`.
- Message persistence.
- Streaming and stream resumption.
- Tool execution and tool-call hooks.
- Custom system prompts and per-turn prompt/tool/model overrides.
- Session context blocks and model-writable memory.
- FTS5 search via Session.
- Sub-agent RPC via `chat()`.
- Programmatic turns via `saveMessages()`.
- Durable accepted work via `submitMessages()` / `inspectSubmission()` / `cancelSubmission()`.
- Durable chat recovery with fibers.
- Workspace file tools backed by DO SQLite, optionally R2.
- Optional sandboxed code execution, browser tools, MCP, client tools, approvals, and dynamic extensions.

Open question answered by `docs/sub-agents.md`: sub-agents are **child Durable Objects / facets colocated under a parent agent**. They have their own isolated SQLite storage, own WebSocket connections, independent in-memory state, schedules, fibers, and chat recovery, but share the parent's machine and are spawned/listed/deleted through the parent. They are not independently placed edge-wide DOs. They communicate with parent/siblings through typed RPC stubs and explicit routing, not shared memory by default.

For our first prototype, Think can probably be the **agent mind loop**. We should still build the **game master / campaign-state supervisor** ourselves using explicit state/event/projector boundaries.

Good framing:

> Think runs the mind. The Game Master owns the world. Our D&D engine governs what each mind can see and what it is allowed to change.

## Capability map for Cloudflare Agent Dungeon

| Need | Does Think provide it? | Source-backed note | What we still build |
|------|------------------------|--------------------|---------------------|
| Agentic loop | Yes | `Think` owns `streamText`, `maxSteps`, tool rounds, message persistence, hooks. Docs call it “full chat lifecycle — agentic loop, message persistence, streaming, tool execution...” | D&D-specific prompts, skills, and turn contracts. |
| Durable identity/state | Yes | Think subclasses are Durable Objects/facets with SQLite-backed Session and Workspace. | Campaign IDs, character IDs, world/module IDs, and mapping to DO names. |
| DM/player prompts | Yes | Override `getSystemPrompt()` or `configureSession()`; `beforeTurn()` can override system per turn. | Actual DM/player skill text and per-agent prompt policy. |
| Tools | Yes | Built-in workspace tools; `getTools()` for AI SDK tools; MCP/client/extensions can merge in. | D&D tools: observe, propose action, reveal info, roll request, consult rules, etc. |
| Tool gating | Yes | `beforeTurn()` can restrict `activeTools`; `beforeToolCall()` can block/substitute/modify. | Hard fog-of-war policy and per-role allowed tools. |
| Streaming monitor | Mostly | `onChunk`, `onStepFinish`, `onChatResponse`, WebSocket chat, sub-agent callback `onEvent`. | Convert Think chunks and game events into a public monitor event stream. |
| Programmatic game turns | Yes | `saveMessages()` triggers a turn without WebSocket; `submitMessages()` durably accepts work. | Game scheduler/turn driver that decides when each actor thinks. |
| Parent/child orchestration | Yes | `subAgent(Child, name)` + child `chat()` streams via callback. Generic sub-agent docs say children are colocated facets with isolated SQLite, own WS connections, and typed RPC stubs. Assistant example uses parent `AssistantDirectory` with child `MyAssistant` facets. | Game Master / campaign supervisor shape, registry gates, and sibling communication policy. |
| Cancellation | Yes | `cancelChat(requestId)`, AbortSignal for local calls, durable `cancelSubmission()`. | Operator UI controls and policy for cancelling a turn vs ending a campaign event. |
| Recovery | Yes | `chatRecovery = true`; fiber recovery; `onChatRecovery`. | Decide when recovered turns are safe for game state, especially if parent monitor/driver vanished. |
| Persistent memory | Yes | Session context blocks with `set_context`, `load_context`, `search_context`; writable memory. | Boundary between private agent memory, campaign source of truth, and public event log. |
| Rules/source lookup | Mechanism yes | Custom tools can call external APIs; workspace can hold files; search context exists. | JoelClaw docs tool first; later Cloudflare Vectorize + FTS. Prevent public corpus leakage. |
| Skills | Partial | Docs say `configureSession()` can add “skills”; source/docs mention context blocks and context-loading tools. | Need inspect Session docs/source next to understand first-class skill semantics. |
| SvelteKit monitor | No | Think examples are React/Vite with `useAgentChat`. | We build monitor app. Can still use raw WebSocket/protocol or Svelte wrapper. |
| ATProto trust/provenance | No, not directly | Think provides DO identity/session/events, not atproto records. | Our typed event/record schema and provenance layer. |

## The shape that fits D&D

### 1. Game Master parent owns the real-time world state

Use a parent Durable Object as the Game Master / campaign supervisor. It owns the campaign/world, which is the canonical stateful representation of the current real-time world:

- campaign metadata
- room/module/world canonical state
- hidden information
- dice ledger
- player registry
- event log
- public monitor projection
- turn scheduling
- visibility projections

The assistant example is the closest source pattern: `AssistantDirectory` owns shared workspace/sidebar/MCP/scheduled work, while child `MyAssistant` facets own their own Think messages/memory/config/extensions.

For us:

```txt
GameMaster[game-id]
  ├─ DMAgent[dm]
  ├─ PlayerAgent[player-a]
  └─ PlayerAgent[player-b]
```

Difference from assistant: shared workspace is dangerous for players. This bit Joel before: if player agents share or can inspect global game state, the game leaks and they know everything about everything. Player agents must be sandboxed/firewalled from canonical state.

### 2. Think child agents own private mind/session

Each DM/player Think agent can own:

- private message history
- role-specific memory/context
- role-specific skills
- role-specific tools
- branch history / recovery

Players should not have direct tools that read canonical campaign state. They get projected inputs from the Game Master only.

### 3. Fog-of-war belongs below prompts

Do not rely on “don’t peek” instructions. Use Think hooks/tools to enforce visibility:

- `beforeTurn()` limits active tools by role and phase.
- `getTools()` only exposes role-safe tools.
- `beforeToolCall()` blocks/substitutes any illegal call.
- Game Master produces a player-visible projection and passes that into the player turn.
- Game mutation tools return proposals/events, not direct writes to canonical state.

This is not only good game design. It is the real agent-systems lesson: agents should be firewalled from data and capabilities they should not harvest or mutate. Prompt trust is not a boundary.

### 4. Agent outputs should be proposed actions

Player agents should produce typed proposed actions. DM agent should adjudicate and reveal. Dice should be a first-class system, not hidden model vibes.

Example flow:

```txt
Game Master emits visible scene to Player A/B
  -> Player Think agents propose actions
  -> Game Master validates proposal shape
  -> Game Master / dice service resolves needed rolls
  -> DM Think agent adjudicates with rules/context tools
  -> Game Master commits canonical event
  -> Public monitor receives sanitized event
  -> Player projections update
```

### 5. Jump straight to a game loop

For a running game, callers should not block on every model turn. `submitMessages()` gives durable acceptance, idempotency, inspection, and cancellation. This is a better fit for scheduled/ongoing gameplay than raw `saveMessages()`.

Earlier recommendation hedged toward `saveMessages()` or `chat()` for simplicity. Updated recommendation: jump straight to a small durable **game loop** and use `submitMessages()` where timeout ambiguity/retry duplication matters. Use child `chat()` only where direct streaming RPC is simpler and safe.

## Useful source excerpts / evidence

### Think explicitly claims the agentic loop

From `docs/think/index.md`:

> `@cloudflare/think` is an opinionated chat agent base class for Cloudflare Workers. It handles the full chat lifecycle — agentic loop, message persistence, streaming, tool execution, client tools, stream resumption, and extensions — all backed by Durable Object SQLite.

This directly answers the big question: Think is intended to own the loop we did not want to invent.

### Think vs AIChatAgent

From `docs/think/index.md`:

- `AIChatAgent` is a protocol adapter; app owns the LLM call.
- `Think` is the opinionated framework; subclasses override `getModel()`, `getSystemPrompt()` / `configureSession()`, and `getTools()`.

For this demo, Think is the better spike because we want to learn its built-in loop before deciding to drop to lower-level primitives.

### Configuration hooks we likely need

From `packages/think/src/think.ts` and docs:

- `getSystemPrompt()` — role/system fallback.
- `getTools()` — server-side AI SDK tools.
- `configureSession()` — context blocks, compaction, search, skills.
- `beforeTurn()` — inspect assembled system/messages/tools/model and override model, system, messages, tools, `activeTools`, max steps, temperature, output, etc.
- `beforeStep()` — per-step overrides.
- `beforeToolCall()` — allow, block, substitute, or change input before tool execution.
- `afterToolCall()` — observe result.
- `onStepFinish()` / `onChunk()` / `onChatResponse()` — stream/analytics/lifecycle events.

These are exactly where D&D lifecycle and monitor events can hook in.

### Sub-agents fit DM/player actors

From `docs/think/sub-agents.md`:

`chat()` runs a full turn on a sub-agent, persists user message, runs the agentic loop, persists assistant response, and streams chunks through callback methods:

- `onStart({ requestId })`
- `onEvent(json)`
- `onDone()`
- `onError(error)`

This is probably enough for a parent `CampaignDirectory` to drive DM/player children and forward stream events to a monitor.

### Programmatic submissions fit ongoing gameplay

From `docs/think/programmatic-submissions.md`:

`submitMessages()` creates a durable acceptance boundary. It returns quickly with a submission record and supports idempotency keys, later inspection, listing, cancellation, and statuses:

- `pending`
- `running`
- `completed`
- `aborted`
- `skipped`
- `error`

This maps cleanly to a turn queue / campaign event loop.

### Assistant example validates parent/child/shared-resource pattern

From `examples/assistant/README.md`:

```txt
AssistantDirectory ("alice")
  ├─ MyAssistant[chat-abc]   [facet]
  ├─ MyAssistant[chat-def]   [facet]
  └─ MyAssistant[chat-ghi]   [facet]
```

The parent owns cross-chat concerns. Children are full Think agents. This gives us a pattern for `CampaignDirectory` + DM/player children.

Important caution from the same example: shared workspace means every chat can see every chat's files. For D&D fog-of-war, that is usually wrong for players.

## Risks / gotchas

### Think is experimental

Docs mark `@cloudflare/think` experimental. Good for a prototype, but expect API churn.

### Skills need another research pass

The docs repeatedly mention Session “skills,” but this pass mostly verified context blocks and context tools. We should read `docs/sessions.md` and relevant Session source before designing DM/player Skills.

### Shared workspace is a fog-of-war footgun

The assistant example’s shared workspace is useful for multi-chat assistants, but dangerous for player agents. For D&D, we likely want:

- DM/global workspace private to DM/supervisor.
- Player private workspace/memory per player.
- Public/revealed projection store.

### Streaming chunks are not game events

Think stream chunks are model/protocol chunks. The monitor needs typed game events, not just token soup. We can observe chunks for drama, but game state should change only through validated events/tools.

### Recovery can duplicate or stale-act if not governed

Think recovery is powerful, but recovered turns need game-specific safety rules. If a turn was orphaned too long, or the campaign state advanced, recovery might be unsafe. Use `onChatRecovery()` or submission inspection policy.

### Public docs/rules leakage

JoelClaw docs can answer OSE queries, but public monitor should show citations/summaries only when safe. Do not expose full private/copyrighted chunks in public events.

## Updated recommendation for the next implementation slice

Build the smallest Think-first **game loop** spike:

```txt
GameMaster DO / top-level Agent
  ├─ DMAgent extends Think
  ├─ PlayerAgent extends Think (player-a)
  └─ PlayerAgent extends Think (player-b)
```

First playable sequence:

1. GameMaster creates a 3-room hidden module seed and dice ledger.
2. DM agent receives a prompt to flesh out the first room using a `consultRules` tool backed by JoelClaw docs API.
3. GameMaster creates player-visible projections for the revealed entrance/room only.
4. Player agents receive only their projection.
5. Each player proposes one typed action through a tool or structured output.
6. GameMaster validates action shape and resolves dice through a deterministic dice service.
7. DM adjudicates with rules/context tools.
8. GameMaster commits canonical sanitized events.
9. Monitor page streams the event log and player-safe/public projections.

Do **not** implement Cloudflare Vectorize, full ATProto repo semantics, or Pi-in-Sandbox in slice one. Keep them as later choices after Think proves or fails the loop.

## Next research questions

1. Inspect `Agent Tools` docs/source to decide whether DM should invoke players via `chat()` or agent tools.
2. Read `docs/sessions.md` and Session source to understand Think “skills” and context blocks better.
3. Inspect Think example server code enough to copy the parent/child routing pattern safely.
4. Decide if monitor uses raw WebSocket, `useAgentChat`, or a separate SSE/firehose endpoint.
